# Every var below is OPTIONAL. Nothing here is required to run the demo —
# each integration degrades to an honest local fallback when unset, and the
# dashboard's status bar shows which mode each one is in and why. See
# README.md for the full explanation of what's live vs. simulated and why.

# --- FalkorDB (graph memory) --------------------------------------------
# Defaults match docker-compose.yml. Only change these if you're pointing
# at a FalkorDB instance that isn't the local docker-compose one.
FALKORDB_HOST=127.0.0.1
FALKORDB_PORT=6379
FALKORDB_GRAPH_NAME=ember

# --- LaserData (live signal ingestion) -----------------------------------
# Unset = local in-process fallback queue (src/lib/laser/fallback.ts).
# The local Apache Iggy container in docker-compose.yml is reachable but
# wire-incompatible with this SDK's handshake (see README.md — it "always
# constructs a VSR client," which reads as LaserData-managed-backend-only).
# Confirmed real: LaserData Cloud has a free tier, no card required — sign
# up at https://laserdata.cloud, the connection string it gives you drops
# straight in here. Format: user:password@host:port.
# LASER_CONNECTION_STRING=

# How often the background firehose scheduler (src/lib/laser/scheduler.ts,
# started once from src/instrumentation.ts) pulls HN/GitHub and writes
# matches onto the graph, in ms. Keeps memory compounding through the demo
# without needing a manual click every time.
# FIREHOSE_INTERVAL_MS=90000

# --- RocketRide (orchestration engine) -----------------------------------
# Unset = local decision engine (src/lib/rocketride/decide.ts), same output
# shape as the real pipeline. Two ways to connect:
#
# 1) Self-hosted engine — point at a host where the engine actually boots.
#    The bundled docker-compose.yml container crash-loops on an upstream
#    onnxruntime-gpu dependency bug (see README.md); untested on native
#    linux/amd64 hardware.
# ROCKETRIDE_URI=ws://localhost:5565
#
# 2) RocketRide Cloud — set an API key instead, and optionally point URI
#    at https://api.rocketride.ai (the client's built-in default). Genuinely
#    connects, and with OPENAI_API_KEY set below, genuinely runs a real
#    completion and reads a real answer back — pipeline.json now ends in a
#    response_answers terminal node (see README.md).
# ROCKETRIDE_APIKEY=

# Injected into pipeline.json's llm_openai node at call time — never
# written to that file, which only ever contains the literal placeholder
# __OPENAI_API_KEY__. Only matters if ROCKETRIDE_APIKEY is also set.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- xAI (Skeptic's independent verification) -----------------------------
# Unset = Skeptic falls back to a deterministic local heuristic (checks the
# signal headline for rumor-flavored language — see src/lib/guild/skeptic.ts).
# Set this to a real xAI API key (https://console.x.ai) to make Skeptic
# genuinely call Grok's Live Search API, scoped to X (Twitter), before
# deciding whether to veto a candidate. This credential is imported by
# exactly one file in this app (src/lib/guild-platform/xai.ts) — no other
# agent can reach it.
# XAI_API_KEY=

# --- Guild.ai (real platform triggers, one API key per council agent) ------
# `guild auth login` is an interactive OAuth flow that can't be completed by
# this app's coding agent, so deploying is the one step only you can do.
#
# The invocation URL is the SAME for every agent in the workspace — it is
# NOT agent-specific, despite what an earlier version of this file implied.
# What's agent-specific is the credential: each deployed agent gets its own
# Guild Trigger, and each Trigger gets its own API key
# (`POST /api/triggers/{triggerId}/api-keys` — confirmed live; NOT
# `/trigger-api-keys`, an earlier guess). The server figures out which
# agent to run from *that key*, not from anything in the request.
# GUILD_WORKSPACE_OWNER=
# GUILD_WORKSPACE_NAME=
#
# One id/secret pair per agent you've deployed — unset any one of these and
# that stage falls back to its own local tier (see each file in
# src/lib/guild/ for what that fallback is). None are required; set only
# the ones for the agents you've actually deployed.
# GUILD_ELIGIBILITY_API_KEY_ID=
# GUILD_ELIGIBILITY_API_KEY_SECRET=
# GUILD_INVESTMENT_ANGLE_API_KEY_ID=
# GUILD_INVESTMENT_ANGLE_API_KEY_SECRET=
# GUILD_CITATION_DRAFT_API_KEY_ID=
# GUILD_CITATION_DRAFT_API_KEY_SECRET=
# GUILD_APPROVAL_API_KEY_ID=
# GUILD_APPROVAL_API_KEY_SECRET=
#
# Legacy: before this app had more than one deployed agent, Skeptic alone
# used unprefixed GUILD_API_KEY_ID/SECRET. Still honored for Skeptic
# specifically if GUILD_SKEPTIC_API_KEY_ID/SECRET aren't set, so an
# already-issued Skeptic key keeps working unchanged — see
# src/lib/guild-platform/client.ts.
# GUILD_API_KEY_ID=
# GUILD_API_KEY_SECRET=
# GUILD_SKEPTIC_API_KEY_ID=
# GUILD_SKEPTIC_API_KEY_SECRET=
#
# Override for the shared URL itself, if app.guild.ai's shape ever changes:
# GUILD_TRIGGER_URL=

# --- Slack (Approval's real motion) -----------------------------------
# Unset = approve() still flips the queue entry to "approved" but sends
# nothing — the UI shows "not sent (no SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL set)". Set this to
# a Slack Incoming Webhook URL (Slack admin -> Apps -> Incoming Webhooks,
# pick a channel) to make approval a real external side effect: the drafted
# re-engagement note posts to that channel the moment a human clicks
# Approve. This is the one place in the app that actually sends something.
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
